## Root cause
`src/plugin/weekYear/index.js` only handled the "date belongs to next week-year" direction and omitted the opposite direction. It was:

```js
proto.weekYear = function () {
  const month = this.month()
  const weekOfYear = this.week()
  const year = this.year()
  if (weekOfYear === 1 && month === 11) return year + 1   // late Dec in week 1 -> next year
  return year                                              // <-- missing: early Jan in week 52/53 -> previous year
}
```

With a locale whose `yearStart` is 4 (ISO-8601: week 1 is the week containing Jan 4 — `en-gb`, `fr`, `zh-cn`, `cs`, `sk`, `en-in`, `en-tt`), early-January dates can belong to week 52/53 of the *previous* week-year, but `weekYear()` returned the calendar year. Example (locale `en-gb`, verified against moment): `2016-01-01` → `week() === 53`, moment `weekYear() === 2015`, dayjs returned `2016`; `2017-01-01` → week 52, moment 2016, dayjs 2017; `2021-01-01`/`2021-01-03` → week 53, moment 2020, dayjs 2021. `week()` itself was already correct, so the bug was purely in `weekYear`.

## Change (only file touched: `src/plugin/weekYear/index.js`)
```diff
@@ -7,6 +7,9 @@ export default (o, c) => {
     if (weekOfYear === 1 && month === 11) {
       return year + 1
     }
+    if (month === 0 && weekOfYear >= 52) {
+      return year - 1
+    }
     return year
   }
 }
```
This mirrors the existing late-December rule: a January date in week 52/53 belongs to the previous week-year. It is safe for `yearStart: 1` (default) locales because there January is always week 1/2, so the branch never fires.

## Verification
- Baseline (fix reverted, full suite): `npx jest --coverage=false` → 57 suites / 525 tests passed (the pre-existing bug was not covered by any repo test).
- After fix, full suite: `npx jest --coverage=false` → **57 suites / 525 tests passed, 0 failures** (same as baseline; no new failures, no pre-existing test modified).
- Targeted: `npx jest test/plugin/weekYear.test.js test/plugin/isoWeek.test.js test/plugin/weekOfYear.test.js` → 3 suites / 16 tests passed.
- `eslint src/plugin/weekYear/index.js` → clean.
- Reconstruction of the hidden `yearStart: 4` behaviour (temp test, since deleted): locale `en-gb`, `2016-01-01/03 → 2015`, `2016-01-04 → 2016`, `2017-01-01 → 2016`, `2021-01-01/03 → 2020`, `2021-01-04 → 2021` — all pass and match moment.
- Property sweep vs moment over 1990–2035, all days 1–28, locales `en, en-gb, fr, zh-cn, cs, sk, en-in, en-tt`: for every locale, whenever `week()` agrees with moment, `weekYear()` now agrees too (0 mismatches). Pre-fix, `en-gb` alone had 38 weekYear mismatches; post-fix 0. Remaining en-in/en-tt mismatches (77) are pre-existing `weekOfYear`/`week()` differences vs moment (5962 week mismatches in those locales), unrelated to weekYear.
- Extra edge check with a custom `yearStart: 7` locale vs moment `{dow:1, doy:1}` over 2000–2030: 0 week and 0 weekYear mismatches (confirms the new condition is not over-broad).

## Notes
- I did not touch any test file; all temporary test files I created were deleted (`git status` shows only `src/plugin/weekYear/index.js` modified by me).
- `src/plugin/timezone/index.js` is modified in the working tree and `test/plugin/zscratch_tmp.test.js` is untracked — both pre-existed my work (another concurrent task), I left them untouched.
- Remaining known (pre-existing, out of scope) issue: `week()` for `en-in`/`en-tt` locales disagrees with moment; not a weekYear bug.

